<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dobruja</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dobruja is the region between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in the south. In antiquity its coast was lined with Greek colonies such as Histria, Tomis and Callatis. In the 14th century it formed an independent principality, from whose ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa</a> it takes its name, and it was then Ottoman until 1878. The southern part passed finally to Bulgaria in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dobruja.jpg" alt="Dobruja" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casino_de_Constanza,_Constanza,_Rumania,_2016-05-27,_DD_01-03_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mircea I (r. 1386–1418) beat the Ottomans in several battles, among them the Battle of Rovine in 1394, drove them from <b>Dobruja</b> and briefly held the Danube Delta and Silistra; he accepted peace in 1417 after Mehmed I had taken Turnu Măgurele and Giurgiu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…governed by appointed officials or held by members of the royal family and powerful boyars, whose estates and immunities grew over time; by the fourteenth century semi-independent lords controlled regions such as <b>Dobruja</b>, and the realm was held together more by personal ties than by a salaried bureaucracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 840–842 the Frankish monk Walafrid Strabo wrote of monks who reported that certain peoples of Scythia, in the <b>Dobruja</b> around Tomis, still spoke the language of the Gothic Bible and used it in their liturgy.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fertile hinterland in southern <b>Dobruja</b> made it a natural centre for grain exports, and it grew alongside the Milesian colonies of Histria, Tomis and Odessos.</blockquote><a href="/state/callatis">Callatis</a></li><li><blockquote>In the breakup of the Second Bulgarian Empire's authority, a local realm emerged in the coastal region of Karvuna, later called <b>Dobruja</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-dobruja">Despotate of Dobruja</a></li><li><blockquote>Milesian colonists founded Histria c. 657 BCE on a peninsula by the sea near the Danube delta, in modern <b>Dobruja</b>, Romania.</blockquote><a href="/state/histria">Histria</a></li></ul></section>
